he app that asks for an answer and resolves with\nthe action chosen. It looks like the operating system, not like the app."],[2277,"**A colour named in `\"tailwind\"` wins over the token of the same name.** With `\"colors\": { \"primary\": \"#3366ff\" }`, every `bg-primary` in the app draws blue — pagination's selected page, a data table's move indicator — while `colors.primary` in JSX stays the theme's citrus. One name, two colours, and the panels drawn by class are the ones that differ. Set the role in `\"theme\"` instead."],[1916,"**A remote session** (`ssh`, a container's exec stream) fills the same two directions."],[1914,"**On the desktop**, a pseudo-terminal: the app spawns the shell in a pty (`portable-pty` in Rust,\na native pty module in TypeScript), reads the master side into `term.write(chunk)` and sends\n`onData`'s payload back to it. The size `onResize` reports is the pty's window size."],[1915,"**On the web**, a WebSocket to a shell service: `socket.onmessage` writes the socket's bytes,\n`onData` and `onResize` send them the other way."],[143,"**this `Alert`** is a notice drawn on the page or in a form, in the app's own surface, read out by\nits `role=\"alert\"`. It takes no presses and asks nothing: the page stays usable under it."],[145,"**this library's `alert-dialog`** is the same question in `Trigger`, `Title`, `Description`,\n`Footer` and `Action`: parts that fill in that system box, so it is the platform's — its look is\nthe operating system's and its content is strings. A confirmation card the app draws itself,\nwith the app's look and content of its own, is `dialog`."],[2171,"`--all` adds every `registry:ui` item, dependencies included."],[2172,"`--dry-run` writes nothing — not even `nsh-ui.json` or the theme — and prints what it would write."],[12,"`-c, --cwd <dir>` is the app or monorepo directory (default: the current directory). `--registry <url>` is a template containing `{name}`; `add`, `list`, `view` and `diff` use the `registry` in `nsh-ui.json` when it is omitted, or the public registry at `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/aningdev/shadcn-nsh/v<version>/public/r/{name}.json` — the one published with the release your CLI came from, so the files match the CLI you are running. `init` uses that public registry. Every command takes `-h, --help`; `shadcn-nsh --version` prints the version."],[36,"`✓` marks an item when any of its files is in the app; the last line counts the items and the installed ones."],[2231,"`add theme` writes `theme.json`, `light.tokens.json`, `dark.tokens.json`, `DESIGN.md`, `lib/ui/semantics.json` and `lib/ui/theme.ts`, the data of the theme `nsh-ui.json` records (`theme-shadcn` writes shadcn's into `themes/shadcn/`). `init` writes the same files and also points each `nsh.json` at `theme.json`. In a monorepo they live under `packages/ui`. Every component depends on it."],[2147,"`add` keeps a file the app changed and reports it; `diff` shows the lines; `add --overwrite` replaces it. `theme.json` and `semantics.json` are the exceptions: one with missing keys is merged, and customized values are kept unless `--overwrite`."],[140,"`AlertDescription` is a frame, not a sentence: a string is drawn in the note's type and ink, and\nanything else is laid out as given — a column of lines, a link, a row of buttons. What the app puts\nthere carries its own ink."],[97,"`AlertDialog` asks one question and waits for the answer; `Dialog` is the app's own card."],[110,"`AlertDialog` owns the open state and the text its parts registered; `AlertDialogContent` calls\n`nsh/base` `AlertDialog.show` when it opens. The box stays\none while it is up: a part that renders again with the alert still open — a page whose layout\nrebuilds its children does — stacks nothing, and the answer belongs to the app that asked."],[104,"`AlertDialogCancel` is the same button with `role=\"cancel\"`; its `id` is `\"cancel\"` unless named."],[1263,"`align=\"end\"` mirrors the row for what you sent: the avatar moves to the end and the column with\nit, so the bubbles inside follow the message — a `Bubble` takes its place from the column unless\nyou give it an `align` of its own."],[291,"`align=\"end\"` puts the bubble on the side of what you sent; `align=\"start\"` (the default) on the\nside of what came in. A bubble is as wide as what it holds, up to 80% of its column; a `ghost` one\nuses the whole width."],[1218,"`alignment=\"bottom\"` on the viewport passes through to the list, so a conversation shorter than the frame sits at the bottom. `indicator` is a mark at the button's top-end corner; `variant` and `size` are the button's (default `secondary` and `sm`)."],[716,"`animation` is `fade` (the card's default), `slide`, or `none`."],[188,"`AttachmentActions` holds what can be done to the file — a remove, a retry — beside the text, or\nin the corner of a vertical attachment. `AttachmentAction` is this library's `Button`, ghost and\nsmall, for a labeled action such as Retry. Remove is an `IconButton` in that row; there is no `onRemove`. Give the icon button a square `style` (`width` and\n`height` 28) so it sits in the chip; the ghost button's own padding reads as a separate pill."],[190,"`AttachmentGroup` is a row of attachments 12 points apart that scrolls sideways when they do not\nfit — the files under a message, the pictures in a composer. The row takes the platform's own feel:\nno snapping, and no scrollbar."],[189,"`AttachmentTrigger` is a pressable over the whole frame, above the media and below the actions:\npressing the file opens it, and under the pointer it shows its own wash. Name it for screen readers."],[223,"`AvatarBadge` sits on the avatar's end-bottom corner. Pass its color; say the presence in `accessibilityLabel`. The avatar does not clip it."],[222,"`AvatarGroup` shows the first `limit` and overlaps them (`-space-x-2`). `AvatarGroupCount` is the overflow circle's text. Size each `Avatar` the same, and size the overflow with `overflowStyle`."],[1537,"`axis=\"vertical\"` stacks the panels, with the handle's hairline along the column."],[244,"`BadgeVariant` is `\"default\" | \"outline\"`: the two the capsule's look has. `BadgeSize` is `\"sm\" | \"default\"`, and `badgeSizes` is the table: `height`, `font` and `pad`, by size."],[963,"`block-start` and `block-end` take a line of their own, above or below the input's: a word over a note, a count under it."],[258,"`BreadcrumbEllipsis` stands for the steps a long trail leaves out. It is named `\"More\"`, so a\nscreen reader says the trail skipped some rather than jumping from one name to the next; give it\n`onPress` and `role=\"button\"` when it opens a menu."],[292,"`BubbleGroup` stacks consecutive bubbles from one sender 8 points apart. Each `Bubble` keeps its\nown `align`."],[1556,"`bubbleMenu` is Tiptap's bubble menu, filled by the app: it sits over the selection, flips below it\nwhere there is no room, and follows the text as it scrolls. **The app decides when it is there** —\nthis one while the caret stands in a link (`isActive(\"link\")`) or while its address is being\nwritten — and draws what it holds. `extendMarkRange(\"link\")` first, then `setLink` or `unsetLink`,\nso the whole link changes and not just the character under the caret; an address written at an empty\ncaret is `insertContent` of a node marked `link`. Closing the field waits a turn (`setTimeout(…,\n0)`) before `focus()`: on the web the Return that submitted it is still being handled, and an editor\nfocused now would take it and write a line break into the link."],[294,"`BubbleReactions` is a pill in the muted fill that hangs three quarters of its height off the\nbubble's `side` edge (`bottom` by default, or `top`), 12 points in from its `align` end. The\nbubble keeps the room the row hangs out on that side, so the next bubble never covers it. Name the\nrow for screen readers: NSH reads each glyph without context otherwise."],[293,"`BubbleText` is a `Text` in the bubble's type and the ink of the variant around it. NSH has no text\ninheritance — the web gives a bubble's ink to whatever its content holds, NSH does not — so content\nthat is not a single string (a list of paragraphs, markdown rendered into text) wears `BubbleText`\nrather than knowing the variant itself. `BubbleContent` draws a string child the same way."],[1769,"`Button`'s `busy` state draws the ring before the label and blocks a second press."],[330,"`buttonGroupLook()` is exported for a group the app lays out itself: `root(orientation)` the group,\n`item(orientation, position)` the join each button wears, `textFrame()` and `text()` the word's box\nand its type."],[324,"`ButtonGroupText` is a word inside the group on the muted fill — a path, a unit, a count — and\n`ButtonGroupSeparator` is the hairline between it and what follows. The separator turns with the\ngroup: down a row, across a column. Join the word as a button would be joined; the separator needs\nno join, and is hidden from a reader (it is the group's own hairline)."],[418,"`CardContent` pads its sides and nothing else: space the fields the way the form needs."],[420,"`CardFooter` is a row that wraps: a long pair of buttons goes to two lines on a phone rather than\nsqueezing both."],[430,"`CardTitle` and `CardDescription` are `Text`, so they take text props (`numberOfLines`, `role`).\n`CardHeader` is a grid of two columns: the title over the description in the first and the action\nat the trailing edge of the first row, whichever order the three are written in.\n`cardLook()` is exported for a card the app draws itself."],[445,"`CarouselPrevious` and `CarouselNext` draw their own icon and size; `side` and `glass` change the\nchrome. Under every theme they are the same floating pill beside the dots; `carouselArrowLook()`\nhas no per-theme form to follow."],[524,"`chevron=\"down\"` points the chevron down and turns it over as the row opens, the way an accordion's\nturns, for a collapsible that reads as a section of one; `along` (the default) points it along the\nrow, the way a folder's does. The row takes more than one child, drawn in one line before the chevron."],[475,"`ChoiceSize` is `\"sm\" | \"default\" | \"lg\"`: 0.8, 1 or 1.25 times the page's control size, shared with `Radio` (`choiceScales` is the table)."],[1504,"`choiceTileLook` is the checkbox module's, shared by the tile radio: import it from\n`@/components/ui/checkbox`."],[815,"`classes` goes over `editorClasses` part by part (an app's classes come after ours; `null` drops ours), and `style` over `editorFrame()`. The font is set with `fontFamily`, `fontSize` and `lineHeight`, which the editor measures its rows in."],[641,"`closeOnSelect={false}` leaves the menu up for a toggle inside it."],[782,"`closeOnSwipe` is on by default — unlike `Sheet`, which does not follow a drag: the drawer is\na phone's panel, so dragging its top edge down past a third of its height (or 800 points a second)\ncloses it, and a scroll inside it hands the drag over at its top. Escape, Android's back and a press\non the scrim close it too; all of them are `nsh/base`'s, and `closeOnEscape` /\n`closeOnBackdropPress` / `closeOnSwipe` turn each one off."],[1935,"`codeBlockActions` puts buttons over each code block's top-right corner: ghost buttons that show\nwhile a pointer hovers the block on a desktop and always on a touch screen. The `copy` action is\nbuilt in — it copies the block's code and shows a check for a moment — so a reply with a command\nneeds nothing more. Other ids are the app's: they are drawn with `renderCodeBlockAction`'s icon and\ntheir press arrives at `onCodeBlockAction`."],[499,"`CodeBlockFooter` is a row under the code, below a hairline: a tool's result and how long it took, or a \"Show more\" button. A string child is a note in the muted ink; other children keep the ink the app gives them."],[496,"`CodeBlockHeader` holds a `CodeBlockTitle`, which keeps to one line, and `CodeBlockActions`, which\nsits at the end. `CodeBlockCopyButton` is a ghost `IconButton`: a press writes `value` to the system\nclipboard and turns the icon into a check for two seconds. Its `accessibilityLabel` reads \"Copy\",\nthen \"Copied\". Put other buttons in `CodeBlockActions` beside it; a wrap toggle is the app's state,\npassed to `wrap`. There is no floating placement: the actions belong in a `CodeBlockHeader`."],[498,"`CodeBlockLine` is a line you draw yourself, with a `variant`: `highlighted` calls it out, `added` and `removed` mark a diff, each with a `+` or `−` that is not selected either. With `lineNumbers` the lines take their numbers in the order they are drawn. A block that shows a whole diff is `DiffView`."],[512,"`codeBlockLook()` is exported for a block the app draws itself: its parts as the styles they wear (`root`, `header`, `title`, `actions`, `content`, `code`, `line`, `gutter`, `marker`, `footer`, `footerText`)."],[1682,"`collapsible` — `offcanvas` (default) leaves the window when it closes; `icon` folds to a strip of\nicons (`sidebarSize.icon`, 48 points); `none` never closes."],[545,"`CollapsingHeader` is a `nsh` View in the library's look; every\nprop of View passes through, and `style` goes over the look's. `CollapsingHeaderBackdrop`,\n`CollapsingHeaderDescription` and `CollapsingHeaderBar` take a View's too, and\n`CollapsingHeaderTitle` a `nsh` Text's; a string child of\n`CollapsingHeaderDescription` is drawn as its text."],[552,"`collapsingHeaderLook()` is exported for a header an app draws itself: the heights as it draws them\n(`heights`), the parts as the styles they wear (`root`, `backdrop`, `cover`, `description`,\n`descriptionText`, `bar`, `title`, `titleClass`) and the ink the picture is drawn in (`ink`)."],[564,"`ColorPanel` lays out the area, the sliders, the fields and the swatches. It has to sit\ninside `nsh/base`'s `ColorPicker`: the panel's\nparts look their picker up from that root, and outside it they log\n`` `<part>` is outside a `ColorPicker`: its color goes nowhere `` and take no input\n(`references/nsh/crates/nsh-base/src/color_picker/mod.rs:59`)."],[563,"`ColorPopover` wraps the same panel in a field trigger. Spread `colorAreaLook()` and `colorSliderLook()` on `ColorArea` / `ColorSlider` when composing your own layout."],[2243,"`colors` maps the roles a component names onto those keys:"],[1791,"`columnVisibility` is the app's. `\"automatic\"` (the view's own starting value, when the prop is left out) shows every column except where iPadOS decides, and the sidebar where the room is compact. `\"all\"`, `\"doubleColumn\"` and `\"detailOnly\"` are the other values. `onColumnVisibilityChange` reports what the user or the system shows instead — the sidebar button, a swipe from the edge, going back from a column's first page on a phone."],[596,"`ComboboxChip` and `ComboboxChips` render only inside `<Combobox>` — as `ComboboxChips` (the field's\nprefix) or anywhere else under that root, including as a sibling of `ComboboxContent`. Outside the\nroot they throw. A chip drawn somewhere else, a summary beside the field, is the app's own: read\n`value` and update it from the chip's press."],[593,"`ComboboxChips` draws the chosen values as chips at the start of the field — where NSH puts a\nmultiple combobox's `prefix` — so the input keeps the query and the field grows down as the chips\nwrap. A chip's label is its item's title, and its remove button drops that value."],[1303,"`createNavStack` is `nsh/base`'s, re-exported so a page's navigation is one import; the routes are\nits state, and `routes` is an accessor, so what reads it follows a push or a pop."],[1023,"`default` draws no surface, for rows split by a separator. `outline` edges the row, for one that\nstands alone. `muted` puts it on a muted fill (`token(\"muted\")`, shadcn's `bg-muted`): a well\nthat reads on a card and on the page, in either appearance, and is still lighter than an edge."],[239,"`default` fills the capsule with the tone's tint; `outline` draws its edge in the tone's color instead."],[240,"`default` is 20 points tall; `sm` is 18, for a chip beside a smaller row — a tool card's 32 pt tile, a list's second line. The label's size and the padding follow the height."],[2112,"`defaultExpanded` opens folders at first."],[761,"`defaultLayout` is the data; `renderTab` and `renderPanel` draw each tab and panel."],[724,"`Dialog` owns the open state. `DialogContent` mounts `nsh/base` Dialog; every base prop passes through it, and base owns the backdrop, the popup host and Escape, back and swipe."],[745,"`DiffHunkActions` takes a `tone` for the backing it fades in over: `diffHunkTone` answers the change's own row (`up` for an added line, `down` for a deleted one), and any of the theme's tones works, read where the backing is drawn."],[748,"`DiffView` defaults `styles` to `diffViewStyles()`; pass your own to change only the parts you name."],[744,"`DiffView` defaults `styles` to `diffViewStyles()`."],[2018,"`disabled` on a `Toggle` locks that one. `disabled` on a `ToggleGroup` locks every toggle in it —\nthe base bar hands its lock down, so no child needs its own `disabled`. A disabled toggle takes no\npress, leaves the arrow keys' walk and reads as disabled to screen readers; it dims to 50%\n(`disabledStyle`)."],[2041,"`disabled` on a `Toolbar` locks the whole bar: no item takes a press, the arrows skip it and it is\none disabled toolbar to screen readers. `disabled` on a `ToolbarGroup` locks that run alone, the\nrest of the bar still reached. Every item the bar knows locks — a `Button`, a `Toggle`, a `Link`,\na menu's or popover's trigger — because the base bar hands its lock down to each\n(`nsh-base/src/toolbar.rs`, \"items\"). A control that is not a bar item keeps its own `disabled`\nand its own behavior inside a locked bar: a `Command` palette, a text field."],[72,"`disabled` on the trigger takes the row's presses itself — for a row that is not an item's own (an\nitem's `disabled` already does that for the header's trigger)."],[766,"`DockArea` spreads `dockClasses` and default tab/panel renderers. Every prop of `nsh/base` DockArea passes through, and so do the types those props, its ref and its callbacks name — a call site reads them from this file, not from `nsh/base`: `DockAreaProps`, `DockAreaRef`, `DockAreaLayout`, `DockRegion`, `DockNode`, `DockPlacement`, `DockPanel`, `DockPanelOptions`, `DockTarget`, `DockDropIndicator` and `DockInfo`."],[843,"`EmojiPanel` wraps `nsh/base` EmojiPicker with gallery classes and chrome. Pass through any base picker prop via extending the shell later; today only the upstream panel props are supported."],[1220,"`EmptyChat` takes the whole viewport (`flex: 1`, centered) and calls `send` with a starter prompt;\n`turnRow` draws a turn with `Message`, `MessageAvatar` and `Bubble`. While the reader is at the end\nthe viewport follows a new turn; while they are not, it waits where they are."],[858,"`EmptyContent` holds the actions under the words: a button, a field, a link. The block takes the\nroom its parent leaves it (`flexGrow: 1`), so a list that ends in an empty state fills its pane."],[886,"`FieldContent` stacks a label and its description (or an input and its error) beside the control\nof a horizontal field. A label inside `FieldContent` keeps the label's width rather than the\nroom left by the control, whatever the field's orientation."],[891,"`FieldError` shows `errors`' messages — as many as there are, each distinct message once — or its\n`children` when they say more. With neither it draws nothing."],[904,"`FieldError`'s `errors` is `Array<{ message?: string } | undefined>`, as a form library reports them\n(a field may collect several). `fieldFamilyLook()` is exported for a field the app draws itself —\nthe same look without the parts; it is not `fieldLook()`, which is the input's well."],[883,"`FieldSet` holds a legend and the fields under it; `FieldGroup` holds the fields themselves, 16\npoints apart. `Field` is one label and its control. A control that carries its own label (a\n`Checkbox` with children, a `Switch` with children) needs no `Field`: put it in the group as it is."],[675,"`fixed: true` pins a column at the leading edge (`\"left\"` and `\"right\"` say which edge) while the\nrest scroll horizontally under it. Fixed columns lead the row, take their width from `width`\n(content cannot set it) and keep their place in the header too; the seam between a pinned column\nand the scrolling body is `fixedEdge` in `dataTableClasses`. Give the table more columns than fit\nthe box to see it work."],[421,"`flush` on `CardContent` drops the side padding and pulls the body through the card's bottom padding, so a picture meets the rim. The card's `overflow: hidden` clips it to the radius. The header stays padded. Off in the terminal."],[2272,"`focusRing(offset)` is the shared keyboard outline: the muted `ring` edge and a 3-point halo of `ring` at 30%. `hovered()` is the pointer's fill, the `hover` token (plus `stateLayer(\"hover\")` when `state.overlay.hover > 0`). `alpha(color, a)` is a `color-mix` of that color with transparency. `colors`, `finish`, `shape` and `style()` are reactive: read them where they are used."],[1411,"`foreground` stays out of the appearance (dark in light and dark alike); `on-media` is the\nwhite the theme keeps for a dark ground. Keep the scale's midpoint opaque: a fill mixed with the\nbackground reads as a dark cell, and every label then wears the light ink, pale tiles included."],[565,"`format` is one of `hex`, `rgb`, `hsl`, `hsb`, `oklch`; the fields' menu reports the reader's pick with `onFormatChange`."],[767,"`groupClass` is applied after the look's `flex-1 bg-inset`. A later background utility replaces the fill, so `groupClass=\"bg-card\"` draws card-colored groups without `groupStyle`."],[1309,"`header` is `nsh/base`'s slot: give one and `NavPage` draws that instead, as wide as the page and as\ntall as it lays out, with the content below it. `navPageLook()` has the words to build one that\nmatches — `header()`, `title()`, `backGlyph()` and `page()`."],[1306,"`headerRight` is the system header's trailing item, and this header's: on a desktop it is where the\npage's own action goes, drawn as given. A page with nothing to put in the header — its stack's first\none, no title, no trailing action — shows none, and its content starts at the top."],[1630,"`horizontal` (the default) runs across its container, one point thick; `vertical` runs down it and\nis as tall as its row, so the row has to have a height (its own, or one its content gives it)."],[1095,"`icon` adds the arrow a site outside the app gets."],[360,"`icon` draws a lucide node in the look's size and tint: the accent while selected, the danger tone as\n`destructive`; in the terminal, which draws no icons, the `accessibilityLabel` is its text. For an\nicon of your own, pass it as `children` and spread `iconButtonGlyph` on it. `iconButtonSizes` is the\ntable, for geometry that has to match a square. A ghost icon button keeps the scale's metrics (28\npoints with 12 of padding at `default`, 32 and 16 at `lg`), so a bare square is `IconButton` with\n`size=\"xs\"`; a bare text button — a Dock header's Hide — is `size=\"sm\"`; there is no separate bare\nvariant."],[355,"`icon` takes a lucide node and draws it before the label, sized and tinted by the variant, the size and the state (a disabled filled button's icon goes muted with its label). The terminal draws no icons, so there it is the label alone."],[934,"`ImageViewer` is a `Dialog` as a page (`dialogPage`) with its body given whole (`scroll={false}`), so every prop of `DialogContent` passes through but `width`, `scroll` and `children`; `style` goes over the page's, and `animation` defaults to `fade`. Gestures are `nsh` View's `pinch` and `pan`; behavior and accessibility are `nsh/base` Dialog's."],[936,"`imageViewerLook()` is exported for a viewer the app draws itself: its parts as the styles they wear (`page`, `ground`, `caption`, `captionText`, `closeCorner`, `close`, `closeHover`)."],[2262,"`init --theme apple` builds the app on Apple's palette; `--theme native` builds it on the default theme and installs both, for `nativeTheme()` to choose between at run time. On macOS 26+ an explicit `macos.backdrop` wins over `glassEffect`, so `apple` states none; it states `ios.backdrop` as the blur for iOS before 26."],[2135,"`init` and `add` copy files into the app; after that they are the app's own. There are three places to change them."],[2159,"`init` writes `nsh-ui.json`, installs the theme and points each `nsh.json` at `theme.json`; in an app it also sets up the `@/` import alias the docs' snippets use — `\"@/*\": [\"./*\"]` in `tsconfig.json`'s `compilerOptions.paths` (writing the file when there is none) and `\"resolve\": { \"alias\": { \"@\": \"./\" } }` in `nsh.json` — leaving values already there alone with a warning. `add` copies the component and its `registryDependencies` (the theme and the shared look files it imports) into the app; from then on the files are the app's own. CLI has every command, flag and default."],[1215,"`initialScroll=\"start\"` opens at the oldest turn and does not follow, for a thread read from the\ntop. Anything else (the default, `end`) opens on the newest."],[977,"`InputGroup` is a view: every `View` prop passes through. `class`, `style` and `disabledStyle` go over its look's."],[961,"`InputGroupAddon` holds an icon, a word or a button at the start or at the end of the group's line (`align`: `inline-start`, `inline-end`). A press on an addon puts the keys in the input, so the magnifier works as part of the field; a press on a button in it is the button's alone."],[964,"`InputGroupButton` sizes a button inside a group: `xs` is a `sm` button (24 points high with a\npointer), `sm` a `default` one (28), and `icon-xs` / `icon-sm` a square of that height. `variant`\nis a button's, `ghost` unless set."],[980,"`InputGroupInput` and `InputGroupTextarea` take every prop of `nsh/base` Input, `ref` included: `value`, `onChange`, `placeholder`, `keyboardType`, `maxLength`, `readonly`, `disabled` and the rest. `InputGroupTextarea` is the multiline one. Their `style` goes over the look's. A `ref` is the control's `InputRef`: `ref.focus()` puts the keys in it from code (a shortcut, a request to focus a draft)."],[970,"`inputGroupLook` is the frame's props and `inputGroupControlLook` the control's, for a `nsh/base` control of your own; `inputGroupAddonLook` and `inputGroupTextLook` are the addon's and its text's. This is what the reference page draws, and what `InputGroup` wraps."],[2020,"`inset={false}` skips the track when a parent `Toolbar` already wears it."],[996,"`invalid`, `readonly` and `disabled` are the single-line field's, and a multiline one takes them the same way. A composer that sends on Return takes `submitBehavior=\"submit\"` and keeps Shift + Return for a break:"],[1021,"`ItemGroup` is a `role=\"list\"` column of rows, `density.gap` apart; `ItemSeparator` is the hairline\nbetween them. `Item` is the row: `ItemMedia` before its content, `ItemContent` for the title and\nthe note, `ItemActions` for its controls. `ItemHeader` and `ItemFooter` are lines across the whole\nrow — a header above the content, a footer under it."],[1025,"`ItemMedia` holds what comes before the content: `default` the app's own (an avatar), `icon` a\nsquare tile with a surface's edge for an icon, `image` a square frame that clips what is put in it.\nAn `Avatar` names its person; an `Icon` is a canvas a reader does not stop at, so the title must\nsay what the icon means."],[2111,"`items` nest; a folder opens with a press or its chevron."],[1053,"`KbdGroup` puts keys side by side, a small gap apart; `Kbd` is one cap — a modifier, a letter, a\nword (`Esc`, `Ctrl`)."],[1216,"`keyExtractor` names a turn's row. The default reads the turn's `key`, then its `id`, then its\nindex; give it one when the data has nothing to name a turn by. A key does two things: it keeps a\nrow's cell and its size as rows come and go, and it is the name `scrollToMessage` takes — a turn\nwith the same id on screen and in the command find each other. Keys must be unique and stable: with\nan index, every append that shifts a row makes the list rebuild it."],[811,"`language` takes a name, an extension or a file name; the editor highlights it and follows its editing rules (comments, brackets, indentation)."],[746,"`layout=\"split\"` puts the old text on the left and the new on the right."],[1142,"`lead` is what sits before the label — an icon, or a mark of the app's. A string child is the row's\nlabel, and the label the touch sheet's back row shows above the nested menu; children drawn as given\nare laid out between the lead and the chevron, which stays either way."],[1789,"`leading` and `trailing` are the header's items. On a desktop they sit in the bar; on iOS and Android they are the system header's leading and trailing buttons."],[1077,"`lib/ui/as-text.tsx` compiles into the app beside it and is a different thing: a `Label` draws\ntype, while a control's name for screen readers is its own `accessibilityLabel`, or the text it\ndraws — the string child `AsText` hands to a `Text`."],[2213,"`lib/ui/icons.tsx` is the app's own after `add`. To use another set, keep `Icon`'s props and change what it draws: the components only ever pass an icon, a size, a tint and a weight."],[2232,"`light.tokens.json`, `dark.tokens.json` and `DESIGN.md` are generated outputs: import `.tokens.json` into Figma Variables as Light and Dark modes, Tokens Studio, or Style Dictionary v4, and let coding agents read `DESIGN.md` for the theme's tokens and design rationale. Because they describe the theme as shipped, editing `theme.json` or `semantics.json` later does not update them."],[497,"`lineNumbers` numbers each line of a string child; `startLine` numbers from a given line, for a snippet from the middle of a file. The gutter is not selected with the code. One trailing newline does not make a line."],[2173,"`list` reads the catalog (`index.json`) and marks an item when any of its files is in the app; `--json` prints the same catalog for tooling. `view` prints what `add` would write, dependencies first, with imports rewritten for the app. `diff` compares the same rewritten content with the app's files; without an item it lists the installed items that differ, with one it prints the changed lines, and it exits 1 when anything differs:"],[1112,"`Marker` is one muted line at the body's size, at least 16 points tall, an icon 8 points before\nthe text. `MarkerIcon` takes a lucide node (`icon`) or a part of its own as children — a `Spinner`,\nfor a status."],[1483,"`marker` is where a limit stands, a percentage along the bar: a hairline crossing the rail and\nstanding past it. The hairline is drawn on the bar's own frame, so the rail comes first in the\nbar's row and a label goes after it."],[1173,"`Menubar` is a row a step over its triggers — the theme's height step under a row, the room either\nside of them and its own 1-point edge — inside a 1-point edge with the card's fill, its triggers 8\npoints either side (`menubarLook().bar`). It places nothing itself: a `MenubarMenu` gives a trigger\nand its popup, and the trigger stands in the bar, not in a box of its\nown."],[1176,"`MenubarItem`, `MenubarCheckboxItem`, `MenubarRadioItem`, `MenubarLabel`, `MenubarSeparator`,\n`MenubarShortcut`, `MenubarGroup` and `MenubarSub` / `MenubarSubTrigger` / `MenubarSubContent` are\n`Menu`'s parts under the bar's names: a row of a bar wears what a row of a menu wears, down\nto its danger tone, its check and its dot. `MenubarShortcut` is `MenuShortcut` (the row's trailing\nnote), and `MenubarLabel` is `MenuLabel`: its child is the title. There is no radio-group part:\n`MenubarGroup` is a `View`, and each `MenubarRadioItem` carries its own `checked`."],[1143,"`MenuButton` is the platform's pull-down menu — `UIContextMenu` on iOS, the context menu a pointer\nopens on the desktops — with its items as data (`MenuButtonItem`: `id`, `label`, `icon`, `checked`,\n`disabled`, `destructive`, `items`, `inline`). The app decides that is the better menu there; a phone\nwithout one (Android) draws `Menu` instead. On the web a menu is always the app's own drawing, so\n`Menu` and its parts are."],[1141,"`MenuItemVariant` is `\"default\" | \"destructive\"`, and `menuItemLabel`, `menuItemNote` and `menuItemGlyph` take the same word."],[1264,"`MessageAvatar` is 32 points round (36 on a touch screen), at the bottom of the row. With no\n`children` it is this library's `Avatar`: `name` gives it initials on an identity hue, `src` a\npicture. An avatar with neither keeps the muted circle that holds the row's alignment."],[1266,"`MessageGroup` stacks messages from one sender 8 points apart. Render an empty `MessageAvatar` on\nthe earlier ones to keep them aligned with the avatar on the last."],[1267,"`MessageHeader` names the sender above the turn, at the start of the row; `MessageFooter` carries\nwhat is under it — a delivery status — on the message's side. A string child is drawn in the look's\ntype."],[1223,"`MessageScroller` is a `View`, so `selectable` passes straight through: a drag over the frame is one\nselection over the rows that are mounted, instead of one per bubble."],[1217,"`MessageScrollerButton` floats 16 points above the newest turn (or below the oldest) in the middle\nof the viewport, and shows only while there is somewhere to go that way. It says \"Scroll to end\" or\n\"Scroll to start\" to screen readers; give it `accessibilityLabel` to say something else."],[1226,"`MessageScrollerItem` is the box one turn sits in, sized by what is in it; `MessageScrollerViewport`\nis the list the turns are in, 32 points apart. What a turn is, is `Message`'s business."],[1224,"`MessageScrollerProvider` shares the scroller with controls outside the frame, such as a list of\nmessage titles: under it, `useMessageScroller()` returns `scrollToMessage`, `scrollToStart` and\n`scrollToEnd`, plus `atStart` and `atEnd` (read those in a binding, like any signal). The turn\ntargeted by `scrollToMessage` is the one whose key is that name; without a matching row the call\ndoes nothing. `MessageScroller` works without the provider, with the hook reading the scroller it is\ninside."],[1225,"`MessageScrollerViewport` calls its `ref` with the `ListRef` beside\nthe one the scroller keeps, so the app can reach the list directly — `scroller?.scrollToIndex({ index: 0 })`,\nfor example."],[995,"`multiline` makes the same field several lines: the content starts at the top, the box is at least one line tall, and Return writes a line break."],[592,"`multiple` toggles an item and keeps the list open; the field shows the chosen titles."],[219,"`name` picks a stable hue via `avatarIdentity`."],[1337,"`NavigationMenu` is a navigation landmark (`role=\"navigation\"`, named `\"Navigation\"` unless\n`accessibilityLabel` says otherwise) as wide as the app gives it, holding a `NavigationMenuList`: a\nrow of items 4 points apart with a pointer (6 on a touch screen), wrapping onto a second line when\nthe window is narrow. An item is its trigger, or a link of its own. A trigger is drawn by its\nitem's content, which is what places it in the bar: a `NavigationMenuTrigger` declared after its\n`NavigationMenuContent` draws nothing."],[1353,"`NavigationMenuItem` and `NavigationMenuTrigger` spread the view props they are given on their own\nview, after the look (`style` included), and merge a given `hoverStyle`, `activeStyle`,\n`focusVisibleStyle` or `disabledStyle` after the look's own; the trigger keeps the pointer's enter\nand leave, which drive the item's delays. `NavigationMenu` and `NavigationMenuList` do the same on the root and the\nrow."],[1342,"`NavigationMenuLink` is `nsh/base` Link in the bar's look: a name 13\npoints medium (15 on a touch screen) and, under it, a description in the muted ink. `active` is the\npage the app is on — the `accent` fill at half, and announced as the current page."],[1319,"`NavStack` and `NavPage` take every prop of\n`nsh/base`'s NavStack and NavPage; `style` goes over the look's. `createNavStack`,\n`NavStackState` and `NavStackChangeEvent` are `nsh/base`'s, re-exported."],[1816,"`note` carries the signed move and `tone` the role it and the line share; the previous close is a\ndashed `Grid` behind the wash, so the day's direction is read at a glance."],[2067,"`nsh/base` takes a hint as a host's `tooltip`; `TooltipContent` alone is that hint."],[2070,"`nsh/base`'s Tooltip is the bubble itself, shown through a host's `tooltip` prop, so the root renders one host view around its trigger. `TooltipContent` mounts `nsh/base` Tooltip; every base prop passes through and `class` goes over `tooltipLook`'s."],[105,"`onAction` reports the `id` of the button pressed (the app's name for it, never the label), or\n`null` when the box closed without one."],[816,"`onHover` reports where the pointer rests: the character, the word it is in, **the word's box on the\npage** (`pageBounds`, what a popup is placed against) and the diagnostics there; `onHoverEnd` says\nit left. The app answers with an `Overlay` anchored to that box, so the card follows the word as the\ntext scrolls and flips below it where there is no room. A card that carries buttons is kept alive\nwhile the pointer is over it — `onMouseEnter` / `onMouseLeave` on the card, a timer that closes it a\nmoment after the pointer leaves — or it vanishes under the pointer on the way to them. A fix\nrewrites the file, which the app owns (`value` / `onChange`)."],[1940,"`onImagePress` reports the image that was pressed as `{ src, alt }`: the shape `ImageViewer` takes. Hold it in a signal, and the viewer is open while it is set."],[1221,"`onLoadMoreAtStart` is the list's own, passed through the viewport: while the reader is within\n`loadMoreAtStartThreshold` viewports of the first row, the scroller asks for an older page and\nprepends it. Rows on screen keep their place, so the reading position does not jump. Guard the call\nwith a signal — it can fire again while a page is in flight."],[723,"`open` owns the state; `onOpenChange` reports the next one and why."],[109,"`open` shows the box and `onOpenChange` reports every change of it — `reason` is `\"triggerPress\"`\nwhen the trigger opened it, `\"select\"` when a button was chosen and `\"cancel\"` when it closed\nwithout one."],[885,"`orientation` defaults to `vertical`: the label over the control. `horizontal` puts them on one\nrow, the label as wide as its text and the control taking the rest."],[888,"`orientation=\"responsive\"` is `vertical` in a narrow window — a phone, or a narrow desktop\nwindow, under 600 points — and `horizontal` above it (`narrowWindow`)."],[187,"`orientation=\"vertical\"` puts the media above the text, 120 points wide, for a strip of pictures.\n`AttachmentMedia` takes a picture drawn inside it: give it `variant=\"image\"` and it fades the media\nuntil the attachment is done or idle."],[323,"`orientation=\"vertical\"` stacks them: the corners at the top and the bottom of the column keep the\ncorners, the sides between them are squared, and one hairline is left between two buttons. A button\nbelow another also drops the raised face's lit top edge, which would read as a white seam."],[1394,"`PaginationVariant` is `\"default\" | \"outline\"`; `paginationItemClass` takes the same word, as\n`PaginationItemClassOptions`' `variant`."],[1419,"`Plot` does not expose its scales or a canvas the app can paint on. The geometry, including the insets the axis labels take, is computed in `nsh/base` (`references/nsh/crates/nsh-base/src/plot/mod.rs`: the plot around line 800, the y insets around 803–812, the y map around 902–926, and `.child` around 1361; `axis.rs` `label_insets` around line 474). Copying that inset in JavaScript would drift from the line, so this library does not add an overlay hook. A wash over a band is `plotWash`."],[1409,"`Plot` takes the rows and the axis; `Area`, `Line` and `Grid` are the marks."],[1317,"`popOnBack` says whether the back button pops; `onBackPress` hears every back — the header's button\nand the platform's (Escape, Alt+Left, the mouse's back button, the swipe). A page with unsaved text\nturns the first off, asks in an alert, and pops only when the answer discards:"],[1436,"`PopoverHeader`, `PopoverTitle` and `PopoverDescription` are layout and text: upstream writes a\npopover's content inline, so give them the app's type with `style`."],[717,"`presentation` hands the dialog to the system: `fullScreen` comes up over the window, `pageSheet` and `formSheet` come up as the system's sheet, with what is behind them still in sight (iOS). The system draws the card there — its edge, its radius, its shadow, its place and its height — so the content wears no card, and the app's `style` starts from a page (`dialogPage`) instead. Desktops and the web ignore `presentation`: there the card stands."],[107,"`presentation=\"actionSheet\"` slides the box up from the bottom (a list on Android). Escape and a\npress outside close it unless `closeOnEscape` / `closeOnBackdropPress` say otherwise; an alert\n(`\"alert\"`, the default) waits for a button."],[1460,"`Pressable` draws nothing of its own: no fill, no padding, no height. It is its content, and a `style` adds whatever geometry the region needs. The theme's lightest hover fill, the press's sink and the keyboard's ring are the whole of its look."],[1484,"`ProgressSteps` draws step marks on the bar, a `ProgressStep` each, with `state` `done`, `current` or `upcoming`. The bar runs from the first mark's middle to the last's, so the row hangs half a mark (`progressStepSize()`) past each end: give the `Progress` that room at its sides. `value` stays the app's (0 at the first step, 100 at the last), as do Back and Next. A done step shows a check that pops in; the current one a ring and a dot; reduced motion lands each at once. A string child is the label under the mark and, with the state, its name for screen readers. Middle labels are centred under their marks; the first starts at its mark's outer edge and the last ends at its, so no label hangs past the half mark. The row is laid out left to right whatever the reading direction, as the bar fills."],[1489,"`progressStepsLook()`, `ProgressStepMark` and `progressStepSize()` are the look's parts, for a row of your own."],[1510,"`RadioVariant` is `\"default\"` (the sunk ring) or `\"tile\"` (the whole surface is the control, this library's own value)."],[693,"`range` on the content; the trigger shows both ends."],[164,"`ratio={1}` is a square: an avatar, a swatch, a cover cut to the same width and height."],[1522,"`RefreshIndicator` is a `nsh` View in the library's look; every prop of View passes through but `children`, and `style` goes over the look's. The pull itself (`refreshIndicator`, `pullValue`, `onRefresh`, `refreshing`) is `nsh` Scroll's. There is no pull on the web, so the chip stays hidden there."],[1524,"`refreshIndicatorLook()` is exported for an indicator the app draws itself: its parts as the styles they wear (`root`, `chip`, `arrow`, `ring`, `glyph`, `tint`). Reduced motion stops the ring's spin; the pull still moves the chip, since it is the user's own hand."],[747,"`renderHunkActions` drops `DiffHunkActions` onto each change; `diffHunkTone` picks the tint its backing needs."],[1391,"`renderItem` draws every item, the ellipsis included, and an ellipsis is handed the run it hides:\n`start`, the first page left out, and `end`, the page after the last. Drawing that item as a `Menu`\nis what makes a ten-thousand-page archive navigable — the pages of the run nearest the one shown,\na First page and a Last page, the rest through the arrows. `disabled` (an offline archive) makes\nevery item inert, the ellipsis with them."],[1538,"`ResizablePanelGroup` spreads `resizableHandleLook` for the axis. Every prop of `nsh/base` ResizablePanelGroup passes through."],[73,"`reveal` defaults on; `start` lines the panel up past a leading icon in the trigger, and `end` keeps the same room after the content."],[1552,"`RichEditor` defaults `classes` to `richEditorClasses` and merges yours into them: a part you leave out keeps the look's, `null` drops it, and a part that is a map of node or mark classes (`nodes`, `marks`) merges name by name, so `classes={{ nodes: { heading: \"text-xl\" } }}` overrides that node and keeps the look's others. Every prop of `nsh/base` RichEditor passes through."],[1554,"`RichEditor` wears `richEditorClasses`; the session comes from `createRichEditor`."],[103,"`role` is what each button means, and the platform places and draws it by that: `cancel` on its\nown, `destructive` in red where the platform has red."],[1574,"`Scroll` hides its platform bar; `Scrollbar` draws the theme's bar for it."],[719,"`scroll={false}` draws the children as given, with no scroll of the shell's: for a body that takes gestures of its own."],[1575,"`Scrollbar.setTheme()` installs the same look on every view that still uses the platform indicator; `null` gives the platform's back."],[813,"`search` is what the editor finds and highlights; `onSearchChange` says what it found — how many\nmatches, which of them the selection is on, or why a pattern is not a regular expression. The editor\nnever sets the search itself (`EditorRef` replaces a match, it does not find one), so the bar that\nedits it is a part: `EditorFindBar` takes the editor's `ref`, **the same object the app hands the\neditor's `search`**, and the `result` the editor reported. What is typed into the bar goes back\nthrough `onSearchChange`; its buttons run the editor's own `findNext`, `findPrevious`, `replace` and\n`replaceAll` (and the bar says how many a Replace all rewrote for a moment)."],[1593,"`SegmentedSize` is `\"sm\" | \"default\"`."],[384,"`selected` (a base prop) shows it as chosen. `iconButtonSizes` is the table: `side`, the square's\nside in points, and `glyph`, the icon's size."],[1609,"`SelectLabel` names the section; the list draws its header."],[58,"`serve` prints the `--registry` URL to use."],[1304,"`setRoutes` swaps the whole list — a route that stays keeps its page and its state — and `remove`\ntakes the topmost page of a route out. `onChange` reports the routes after a page is added."],[11,"`shadcn-nsh@latest` copies registry items into an existing NSH app. The component pages use `npx shadcn-nsh@latest add <name>`; this page is the command reference."],[619,"`shortcut` is drawn with a pointer in `kbd`'s key cap, the library's one (`add command` brings it); `checked` marks the row in effect."],[1753,"`showValue` lays the value beside a horizontal rail (a 42-point column) or above a vertical one.\nA string of your own replaces the formatted number and keeps that place."],[1680,"`side` — `left` (default) or `right`: which edge it stands on."],[784,"`side` takes any edge of the panel (`\"bottom\"` unless set). A drawer from the left or the right keeps\nthe panel's full height and its inner corners on the card's radius."],[1678,"`Sidebar` is a column as wide as the sidebar (`sidebarSize.width`, 256 points) on the sidebar's fill,\nits edge against the page, holding a `SidebarHeader`, the groups in a `SidebarContent` that scrolls\nwhen they do not fit, and a `SidebarFooter`."],[1783,"`sidebar`, `content` and `detail` stay props, and each one is a `NavStack`. The platform finds that stack in the slot, so these parts are the pages inside it, not wrappers around it. Leave `content` out for two columns."],[1698,"`SidebarGroupLabel` names a group in 32 points of quiet type; folded to icons it takes no room and\ndraws nothing, and stays in the tree for a screen reader. `SidebarGroupAction` stands at its trailing\nedge — a search, a settings — 20 points square. `SidebarGroupContent` takes the rows, as wide as the\ngroup."],[1684,"`SidebarInset` is the page, filling what is left of the window: the page's own fill, or in the `inset`\nvariant a card with its corners 8 points from the column's card and from the window's edges. Room for\nthe header of the page is the app's: a row with the trigger, a `Separator` (`orientation=\"vertical\"`,\n16 high), where you are, and what it holds."],[1694,"`SidebarMenuAction` is a 20-point square at the row's trailing edge, in `SidebarMenuItem`, and\n`SidebarMenuBadge` a pill there — a count, a short word, its numbers tabular. The item keeps the room\nthey need, so the row's name stops short of them; both on one row, the action steps in by the\nbadge's width rather than drawing over it."],[1689,"`SidebarMenuButton` is a row of `SidebarMenu`, inside a `SidebarGroupContent` of a `SidebarGroup`\nthat a `SidebarGroupLabel` names: the theme's row high (`controlSize.row`), its label the `body`\ntype role, the pointer's fill under it, the `sidebarAccent` fill when `isActive` (the page the app\nis on), the keyboard's ring, and 50% while `disabled`. It takes a press (`onPress`), or it is a\nrow of its own; no `href`, as a navigation menu's link has none."],[1699,"`SidebarMenuSkeleton` is the skeleton's blocks where an icon and a name will be: an icon's box when\n`showIcon`, then a line of text as long as `width` says (70% when it says nothing). A length made up\nper mount is a picture that changes between runs, so this one is told."],[1696,"`SidebarMenuSub` holds the rows under a row that opens them — `SidebarMenuSubItem` and its\n`SidebarMenuSubButton`, the height step under the row (`controlHeight.md`). It is 14 points in from\nthe row above it, with a hairline down its start in the sidebar's edge color, and it hides while the\ncolumn is folded."],[1685,"`SidebarProvider` owns it: `open` (controlled) or `defaultOpen` (default `true`), reported by\n`onOpenChange({ open, reason })` — `triggerPress`, `railPress` or `shortcut`. Nothing is written\ndown: storage is the app's, which usually keeps the state where it keeps the rest of its settings\nand passes it back as `open`."],[1503,"`size` is `ChoiceSize` (`\"sm\" | \"default\" | \"lg\"`: 0.8, 1 or 1.25 times the page's control size), shared with `Checkbox`."],[185,"`size` is `default` (40-point media, the body's type), `sm` (32, the note's) or `xs` (28, the\nnote's, in a chip's corners)."],[1843,"`size` is the knob: `default` takes the theme's `icon.md`, `sm` its `icon.sm`, and the track is\nthat knob plus 2 points of room on every side."],[1536,"`size` is the panel's current width; `onResize` reports the drag."],[1690,"`size` is the row's step — `default` the theme's row, `sm` the height step under it, `lg` a team's\nswitcher or a user's row, 48 points where the row is a pointer's (a touch screen's row is already a\nfinger's height) — and `variant` is its look: `default`, bare until the pointer is over it, or\n`outline`, a card of its own on the column."],[362,"`size` is the scale (28 points at `default` with a pointer, 40 on a touch screen); `side` is an exact diameter where the scale is off."],[1587,"`size` is the scale: `default` is the theme's `height.md` high (28 points with a pointer in the\nlibrary's themes, 40 on a touch screen) and `sm` its `height.sm` (24, 32)."],[1768,"`size` is the theme's icon tier, so the ring follows the pointer: 12, 16 and 20 points with a\npointer, 16, 20 and 24 on a touch screen. `side` takes an exact size in points where the scale's\ndoes not fit."],[469,"`size` multiplies the page's control size: `sm` 0.8, `default` 1, `lg` 1.25 (`ChoiceSize`)."],[1024,"`size=\"default\"` has room for a title, a note and a control; `size=\"sm\"` takes four points off each\nside for a single line."],[495,"`size=\"sm\"` has tighter padding and smaller code, for the output of a tool."],[1755,"`Slider` is `nsh/base` Slider wearing `sliderLook` and `sliderFill`: every base prop passes through, and the look's classes and styles are defaults the caller can override."],[392,"`SplitButtonSegment` takes every prop of `nsh/base` Button; a string child is the label, and its name for screen readers."],[1792,"`SplitView` takes every prop of `nsh/base`'s SplitView; `style` goes over the look's. A column page takes every prop of `nsh/base`'s NavPage; `style` goes over the column's. `ColumnVisibility` is `nsh/base`'s, re-exported."],[1788,"`SplitViewTrigger` hides the sidebar (`\"doubleColumn\"`) or shows every column (`\"all\"`). It does not show or hide anything itself: the app sets `columnVisibility`, the same value the split takes. The glyph stays `colors.mutedForeground`; the button is selected while the sidebar is showing. iOS draws the system's sidebar button, so leave this one out there."],[220,"`src` is the picture; `name`'s initials cover it while it loads, and stay if it never arrives."],[186,"`state` says how the attachment is doing: `idle` (not sent yet, its edge dashed), `uploading`,\n`processing`, `done`, and `error` (the danger tone through the frame, the media and the\ndescription). Put a `Spinner` in the media while it travels."],[1814,"`StatGroup` wraps several stats onto one row. A stat with no `width` shares that row. A hairline above a chart's figures is the group's class, not the default."],[2090,"`status=\"error\"` takes the destructive role for the title and the chevron; tint the icon with `colors.destructive` yourself, since an `Icon` draws in the tint it is given. A failed call opens onto its log in a destructive `CodeBlock`."],[1934,"`streamingCaret` marks the end of text still arriving; `textViewCaretClass` is the caret's look."],[722,"`style` goes over the card's chrome, `backdropStyle` over the scrim."],[1438,"`style` goes over the surface: a panel that brings its own padding, such as an emoji panel, drops the\npopover's."],[1611,"`style` on `SelectTrigger` is the trigger's box in the app's layout: the properties that place it\nwhere the app puts it — `flex`, `flexGrow`, `flexShrink`, `flexBasis`, `width`, `minWidth`,\n`maxWidth`, `alignSelf` and the margins — go on the view around the trigger, so it grows, shrinks\nand keeps its distances like any other view. The rest of the style (the fill, the edge, the\npadding) stays on the trigger itself."],[1851,"`switchLook` gives the track and knob classes to a `nsh/base` switch of your own; `switchHitSlop` gives the touch target."],[1860,"`SwitchSize` is `\"sm\" | \"default\"`."],[1873,"`TableHead`, `TableCell`, and `TableCaption` render strings with muted or foreground text."],[1872,"`TableRow` accepts `selected` for the `rowSelected` fill."],[1882,"`tableRowClass` is the row's color transition, for a row of your own."],[698,"`testID` finds the trigger in UI tests, and `accessibilityLabel` names it when the\ncontent does not name the field. The root owns the open flag and the value;\n`DatePickerContent` mounts `nsh/base` DatePicker\nand every base prop passes through except those. `datePickerLook` fills the popup, the scrim, the calendar and the time's row by default."],[1941,"`TextView.parse`, `parseHtml`, and `plainText` are re-exported from base. Every prop of `nsh/base` TextView passes through."],[1933,"`TextView` merges `textViewLinkClasses` into `classes` by default."],[933,"`TextView` reports the image that was pressed (`onImagePress`) in the shape the viewer takes; see **Images that open** there."],[2170,"`theme.json` and `lib/ui/semantics.json` are merged by key: the app keeps its colors and semantic values and gains any tokens, style keys, or finishes it lacks. Customized theme files stay as they are."],[2140,"`theme.json` holds the colors for both appearances, the three `radius-*` layer lengths, and font families; `lib/ui/semantics.json` holds style groups and surface finishes (`shared` + sparse `light`/`dark`), referencing tokens with `{token}`. Every component reads them through `lib/ui/theme.ts`."],[2233,"`theme.json` is a shadcn theme: `cssVars.theme` holds the radii and the accent's raw values, `cssVars.light` and `cssVars.dark` hold the same semantic keys for the two appearances. The core keys are shadcn's (`background`, `foreground`, `card`, `popover`, `primary`, `secondary`, `muted`, `accent`, `destructive`, `border`, `input`, `ring`, `chart-1` … `chart-5`, `sidebar*`). Upstream adds the roles the gallery needs:"],[2390,"`theme.json` states one corner radius per layer in `cssVars.theme` — `radius-control` (in-page\nelements), `radius-container` (cards, menus, popovers, toasts) and `radius-modal` (dialogs, sheets) —\nand `semantics.json`'s `shape.*` references them with `{radius-…}`. `shape.corners.button` makes\nbuttons capsules (`apple`, `material`) or gives them `control` (the rest). A surface nested in\nanother takes the concentric corner, `rounded(outer, inset)`. Every corner that reads a theme radius\nuses `shape.curve` (`\"continuous\"` or `\"circular\"`), which `rounded()` applies in one place."],[2265,"`theme.json`'s `cssVars.theme` block defines three corner radii, one per layer: `radius-control` (in-page elements: fields, rows, chips, badges, wells, colour areas, keycaps), `radius-container` (cards, menus, popovers, rich tooltips, list panels, toasts) and `radius-modal` (dialogs, sheets, drawers). shadcn's own `radius` slot equals `radius-control`. In `semantics.json`, `dimension.shape.*` references them with `\"{radius-…}\"`, and `shape.corners.button` (`\"full\" | \"radius\"`) says whether buttons are capsules. `shape` resolves each role reactively via `token()`:"],[1968,"`TitleBar` draws nothing where the app has no window of its own — a browser page, a phone — and\n`WindowControls` are inert there: the bar is a window's, and there is none."],[1980,"`TitleBar` is `TITLE_BAR_HEIGHT` (40 points) tall, or the height the system's title bar has, on\nthe inset fill with a hairline under it; its children start `12` points after the system's buttons.\nA traffic light is `TRAFFIC_LIGHT` (12 points) wide, in macOS's own close, minimize and zoom hues wherever the theme goes (`lightTone`, with glyphs in `lightInk` and the disc's `lightEdge`), and `CAPTION_BUTTON` (46) is a caption cell's width; the close cell\nwashes the destructive tint while the pointer is on it. `WindowResizeEdges` draws eight strips —\n`5` points along the edges, `12` at the corners — as the last child of the window's root.\n`titleBarLook()` is exported for a bar the app lays out itself."],[1989,"`toast` follows sonner. It returns an id. The same id replaces the toast that held it. `update` changes one where it is, which is how a loading note becomes its result — sonner does that by calling `toast` again with the same id, and this queue also has `toast.update` so the entrance does not restart. `progress` (0 to 1) is the bar under the text; sonner has none."],[1998,"`toastLook`, `toastTitle`, `toastText`, `toastCloseLook`, `toastActionLook` and `toastActionLabel` are the look's parts, for content of your own."],[2027,"`toggleLook`, `toggleLabel` and `toggleGlyph` are the look's parts, for a pressable of your own.\n`toggleSizes` is the table a size reads — `side`, `icon` and `text` from the theme's height, icon and\ntype scales, its `pad` the toggle's own (compact with a pointer, roomier on a touch screen) — so a\nbar's own geometry can match a tool's (`toggleSizes.default.side`)."],[238,"`tone` is the theme's `Tone`; a function is read where the badge is drawn, so a value the app changes follows."],[2051,"`toolbarTrack({ vertical })` (`@/lib/ui/tool`) and `toolbarRule(vertical)` are the look, for a bar of your own."],[2100,"`traceLook()` is exported for a trace the app draws itself: its parts as the styles they wear (`root`, `line`, `icon`, `title`, `description`, `collapsible`, `trigger`, `chevron`, `content`)."],[1813,"`unit` sits beside the value, smaller and muted. `note` is a line under it. shadcn/ui has no stat, so the names follow the figure a chart shows: a value, its unit, and a note."],[1686,"`useSidebar()` is what the parts read, and what an app reads inside the provider: `state`\n(`\"expanded\"` or `\"collapsed\"`), `open`, `openMobile`, `isMobile`, `setOpen`, `setOpenMobile` and\n`toggleSidebar`. Every value is live: read it in a JSX binding and it follows the provider."],[401,"`value` is an ISO date; with `range` it is `[start, end]`."],[74,"`value` is the open items; `onChange` reports the next list."],[1681,"`variant` — `sidebar` (default) its own edge against the page; `floating` a card standing in the\nwindow's air, 8 points of room around it; `inset` a card on the sidebar's ground, with the page's\nown card beside it on that same ground, 8 points from it and from the window's edges."],[290,"`variant` changes the frame. `default` is the accent's fill and ink — what you send; `secondary`\nthe raised neutral; `muted` a quieter fill; `tinted` the accent as a soft wash; `outline` a\nhairline around the page's fill; `ghost` no frame at all, for assistant text that takes the whole\nwidth; `destructive` for a turn that failed. Every fill keeps a 1-point edge, transparent where it\nhas no color, so the box is the same size."],[1114,"`variant=\"border\"` keeps the inline layout and closes the line with a hairline 8 points under it,\nfor a row that separates what follows."],[859,"`variant=\"default\"` is the media as the app draws it — an `Avatar`, an illustration, an `Icon`\ndrawn large — with no box around it. `variant=\"icon\"` is the square of the muted fill with a\nsurface's corners, an icon of `EMPTY_ICON` (24 points) in it."],[494,"`variant=\"destructive\"` puts the block in the danger tone: the edge, the title and the code. Use it for errors and failed output."],[1140,"`variant=\"destructive\"` puts the danger tone on the row's label, its note and its icon; `default` is the row as it is."],[138,"`variant=\"destructive\"` puts the whole notice in the danger tone: the title and the icon on it,\nthe note at nine tenths. The parts under the root wear it without being told — the root hands the\nvariant down."],[1912,"`variant=\"ghost\"` draws no fill, no edge, no padding and no corners: the app's own surface is the\nframe, and the terminal covers it."],[1610,"`variant=\"outline\"` is a trigger that is not a form's field: the raised finish instead of the\nfield's well."],[1113,"`variant=\"separator\"` centers the label between two hairlines, each taking the room left on its\nside. Use it for a date, a compaction, or the start of a section."],[672,"`width`, `align` and `sortable` are per column."],[2127,"<ComponentsIndex kind=\"official\" />"],[2128,"<ComponentsIndex kind=\"own\" />"],[658,"<CoverageTable group=\"base\" />"],[659,"<CoverageTable group=\"primitives\" />"],[657,"<CoverageTable group=\"shadcn\" />"],[2308,"<ThemeFinishes id=\"apple\" />"],[2323,"<ThemeFinishes id=\"default\" />"],[2338,"<ThemeFinishes id=\"material\" />"],[2353,"<ThemeFinishes id=\"shadcn\" />"],[2368,"<ThemeFinishes id=\"solid-glass\" />"],[2383,"<ThemeFinishes id=\"winui\" />"],[2391,"<ThemeIndex />"],[2303,"<ThemeInstall id=\"apple\" />"],[2318,"<ThemeInstall id=\"default\" />"],[2333,"<ThemeInstall id=\"material\" />"],[2348,"<ThemeInstall id=\"shadcn\" />"],[2363,"<ThemeInstall id=\"solid-glass\" />"],[2378,"<ThemeInstall id=\"winui\" />"],[2302,"<ThemeIntro id=\"apple\" />"],[2317,"<ThemeIntro id=\"default\" />"],[2332,"<ThemeIntro id=\"material\" />"],[2347,"<ThemeIntro id=\"shadcn\" />"],[2362,"<ThemeIntro id=\"solid-glass\" />"],[2377,"<ThemeIntro id=\"winui\" />"],[2304,"<ThemeTokenLead />"],[2319,"<ThemeTokenLead />"],[2334,"<ThemeTokenLead />"],[2349,"<ThemeTokenLead />"],[2364,"<ThemeTokenLead />"],[2379,"<ThemeTokenLead />"],[2306,"<ThemeTokens id=\"apple\" group=\"extension\" />"],[2307,"<ThemeTokens id=\"apple\" group=\"shared\" />"],[2305,"<ThemeTokens id=\"apple\" group=\"slots\" />"],[2321,"<ThemeTokens id=\"default\" group=\"extension\" />"],[2322,"<ThemeTokens id=\"default\" group=\"shared\" />"],[2320,"<ThemeTokens id=\"default\" group=\"slots\" />"],[2336,"<ThemeTokens id=\"material\" group=\"extension\" />"],[2337,"<ThemeTokens id=\"material\" group=\"shared\" />"],[2335