here the provider draws and gone with it. The rail at the column's inner edge takes a press\ntoo: it is 8 points wide (16 wide to a press, past its bounds with `hitSlop`) and shows a hairline in\nthe sidebar's edge color while the pointer is over it. That hairline follows the pointer through the\nclass language (`group-hover:`), not a signal, so passing the cursor over the rail commits no frame;\nthe pointer has to be on the 8-point strip itself, not only in its `hitSlop` margin. `SidebarTrigger` is the button that usually\nsits in the page's header."],[2124,"83 components, one page each: Installation, Usage, an optional Composition,\nExamples and API. They are grouped in two: Base, aligned with an NSH element,\nand Enhanced, this library's additions with no NSH element of their own.\nBehavior, keyboard support and the full unstyled props live on\nnsh.dev/elements; each page links its base element and the shadcn/ui\ncomponent it stands in for."],[502,"A `CodeBlock` in a card flush against its edges takes its surface off with `style`: the block's edge and corners go, a hairline stays above."],[1390,"A `compact` pager keeps Previous, a child label, and Next on one row."],[1846,"A `Field` in a row puts a title and its description at the start, `FieldContent` takes the room\nthe control leaves, and the switch sits after them."],[890,"A `FieldSeparator` is a hairline between fields; with children it is a hairline broken by its word."],[1061,"A `Kbd` is one line high with a minimum width (20 points with a pointer, one Cell row in the\nterminal), a hairline edge and the `muted` fill; its name is small type in the muted ink. There is\nno axis. `kbdLook()` is exported — `kbd`, `text` and `group` — for a cap or a row the app draws\nitself."],[1345,"A `NavigationMenuTrigger` or `NavigationMenuLink` that is not a string is drawn as given, so an icon\ncan lead a name."],[1938,"A `pattern` claims inline text instead (the next example), and `tag` and `container` claim a tag\nor a wrapping block. `renderCustom` is called for every custom node in the document, so branch on\n`node.name` when the app claims more than one shape."],[2089,"A `TraceLine` with `onPress` is a pressable: it takes the text color under the pointer and the focus ring with the keyboard on it. Use it for a source that opens a page."],[1410,"A `Treemap` or `Bar` tile filled with a chart role takes two inks: a light tile reads a dark\nlabel, a dark one a light label. `nsh/base/plot` sets `data-dark` on a label whose cell's colour\nis dark, so `labelClass` names both:"],[1334,"A bar item that is a link"],[1974,"A bar of the app's own takes `desktop={{ windowDrag: true }}` to drag the window, and pads by\n`titleBar().left` and `titleBar().right` so its content clears the system's buttons."],[1555,"A bar over the document, every control reading the caret: the block it stands in, the marks it\ncarries, the list around it. The session re-reads on each `revision`, so `isActive` and `can` are\ncurrent when the bar draws; a control whose command cannot run where the caret stands is `disabled`\n(`can`), and a press runs the command and puts the caret back with `focus()` — or the document loses\nit to the button. `command` builds the Tiptap commands by name; the list indents are\n`sinkListItem` and `liftListItem` over `listItem`."],[2050,"A bar without `ToolbarOverflow` behaves identically to a static bar. In a bar with `ToolbarOverflow`\non a horizontal non-terminal surface, unmeasured items stay visible rather than blocking folding.\nBecause `nsh/base`'s `Button`, `Toggle` and `Link` do not forward `onFocus`/`onBlur`, `Toolbar` does\nnot expose `onFocusIn`/`onFocusOut`, and `nsh/base`'s roving focus marks non-stop items\n`focusable={false}` (`roving.rs`), focus cannot automatically move to `\"⋯\"` when a focused tool\nfolds while an earlier tool remains visible."],[1732,"A block of content"],[1929,"A block of your own"],[853,"A block that says one thing"],[1650,"A bottom sheet can rest at heights instead of fitting its content: `detents` names them (`\"medium\"`\nis half the screen, `\"large\"` its room), and a drag or a press on the grabber moves between them. Pass\n`detent` and `onDetentChange` to own which one it rests at, and leave `closeOnSwipe` on to let a\ndownward swipe below the smallest close it."],[1648,"A bottom sheet has a grabber at its top: `nsh/base`'s own handle, which screen readers adjust to\nchange the detent and which moves to the next larger one when pressed, drawn in the theme's look\n(`sheetGrabber`). It is the handle itself, not a picture of one, so a sheet never shows two. `grabber`\noff shows it; `grabberAccessibilityLabel` names it (\"Sheet\" by default)."],[154,"A box as wide as its column and as tall as its ratio says."],[332,"A button, an icon button, a round button, and a split button."],[681,"A calendar in a popup from a field trigger, on the shared listbox look."],[2088,"A call goes `pending` (dimmed), `running`, then done (no status), and opens onto what it did. The result is a `CodeBlock`: a search, a weather lookup and a code run are the same `Trace` with a different body — a list of `Item`s, a card, a `CodeBlock` — not a card of their own. Disable the trigger until there is something to show."],[1416,"A candle is a `Bar` from the open to the close and a `Rule` from the low to the high, both colored\nby a `\"dir\"` category. The volume is a second `Plot` under the price one, sharing its band scale so\nthe bars line up, with its own right axis:"],[1054,"A cap is a view, not a span of a paragraph: in running text the sentence is a row of `Text` and\n`KbdGroup`, wrapping together."],[700,"A card dialog with a trigger, content, title, description, footer and close."],[419,"A card takes the width its style gives it; `flexGrow` with `flexBasis` puts two across a window\nand one across a phone."],[846,"A centered block that says there is nothing here yet, and what to do about it."],[1204,"A chat"],[636,"A checkable row and its neighbours"],[642,"A checkable row keeps its mark's room whether it is checked or not, so its label stays put as it\nis toggled. `inset` gives the plain rows of the same menu that room too, and their labels line up\nwith it. `ContextMenuLabel` and `ContextMenuSubTrigger` take it as well."],[521,"A chevron that turns over"],[594,"A chip can take a mark at its start (`lead`) or keep its value against a stray press\n(`showRemove={false}`); a function child draws each value's chip instead of the default one:"],[161,"A color block"],[317,"A column"],[674,"A column fitted to its content takes `fitPadding` for the room the drawing adds beside the text: a\ncolumn of this look sets **16** — 8 points before a cell's text, 4 after it, and the 4-point gap\nbefore a header's sort button."],[834,"A composer keeps the panel behind a `Popover` on the field's leading addon. Choosing closes the\npanel and puts the emoji at the end of the draft; `base` (the emoji without its skin tone) is what\nleads the recents, so the tab that holds it opens first the next time."],[82,"A confirmation of the platform's own."],[1219,"A conversation starts with an empty frame: keep the greeting and its suggestions inside the\n`MessageScroller` until the first turn arrives, so the frame holds its height and the composer under\nit never jumps. `MessageScrollerProvider` shares the scroller with that composer, and the send\nbutton is a `RoundButton` beside an `Input`."],[1194,"A conversation that keeps up with itself: new turns follow the newest message while you are at the end, and wait when you are not."],[2142,"A copied `semantics.json` writes every field of every finish, so nothing here is guessed: a field the theme does not care about carries its neutral value — `material: null`, `backdropFilter: \"none\"`, `elevation: null`, `gradient: null` (a flat fill), the pressed pair `null` (pressing only dims), and no colour of its own on the top or bottom edge, which then takes the finish's `border-color`. In the library's own sources those neutrals are written once in `packages/theme/tokens/base/style.shared.tokens.json`; in a copied app they are simply there. If you add a finish role, copy every field of a neighbouring role and change the ones that differ, and state device-dependent sizes twice, under `pointer` and `touch`, with the same keys."],[1967,"A desktop window with `titleBar: { style: \"hidden\" }` keeps the system's window buttons and leaves\nthe rest of the bar to the app: `TitleBar` draws it, drags the window, and pads its children clear\nof the buttons (`titleBar()`'s `left` and `right`)."],[1133,"A destructive item"],[1470,"A determinate or indeterminate progress bar."],[720,"A dialog the app fills the window with — a lightbox, an editor — passes `dialogPage()` as `style`: it takes the card's edge, radius, shadow, padding, width and height cap off and fills what the dialog gives it."],[960,"A disabled group disables what it holds and dims it."],[514,"A disclosure row that reveals a panel beneath it."],[1415,"A donut is a `Pie` with an `innerRadius` in percent and a small `padAngle`; the figure in its hole\nis a view placed over the plot, not a label the plot writes:"],[1821,"A dot and label for quiet state in rows or headers."],[1645,"A draft the footer applies"],[785,"A drawer's actions stack — that is what a finger reaches on a phone — so `DrawerFooter` is a column\nand each action is as wide as the drawer. A row of destinations is a `DrawerClose` each, so tapping\none closes the drawer after its `onPress`."],[2084,"A failed step"],[1257,"A failed turn"],[940,"A field's frame around an input and what sits beside it — an icon, a unit, a button."],[1815,"A figure whose value moves with a series reads better with the series beside it: the `Stat` gives\nthe name, the value and the change in the same tone as the line, and a `Plot` at 104 × 38 draws the\ntrend without axes or a pointer — `interactive={false}`."],[2169,"A file the app changed is kept and reported: `kept components/ui/button.tsx, differs (run diff)`. `--overwrite` replaces it and prints the change: `overwrite components/ui/button.tsx (+2 -1)`."],[2168,"A file the app does not have is written; one identical to the registry's is skipped."],[29,"A file the app lacks is written, one identical to the registry's is skipped, and one the app changed is kept unless `--overwrite`. Without `--overwrite`, `theme.json` and `lib/ui/semantics.json` gain only the keys they lack (keeping the app's own colors, style values and finishes). `theme` — named, or the dependency every component has — is the theme `nsh-ui.json` records, so in an app on `theme-shadcn`, `add theme --overwrite` writes shadcn's data back, not the default one's."],[171,"A file, a picture or a link carried by a message: its media, its name, how it is doing, and what can be done to it."],[1754,"A fill that starts at the rail's start (`from` at or below 0) stays nsh's own run, from the\nminimum to the thumb. A fill that starts later sets its own edges. Name the farther end first\nwhen the span reaches back to the start, so a band lit from the middle still places itself at\nthe minimum:"],[2245,"A finish is light and depth: what a surface is filled with, and how it sits above what is behind it. There are fourteen — eleven layers a component can be drawn on, and three states — and a finish states the same fields in every theme: `background`, `foreground`, `shadow`, `material`, `gradient`, `backdropFilter`, the three border colours, the pressed pair and `elevation`. A field a theme leaves out is not missing — it takes a neutral value, written once in the base token set (`tokens/base/style.shared.tokens.json`) and compiled into every theme's `semantics.json`: `material: null` (no platform material), `backdropFilter: \"none\"`, `elevation: null`, `gradient: null` (flat), no colour of its own on the top or bottom edge, and the pressed pair `null` (pressing only dims). In the DTCG sources those neutrals are written `\"none\"` in the finish group's `$extensions`, since a token's `$value` cannot be `null`; a token a theme writes wins over it. `semantics.json` keeps `finishes.shared` (fields identical in both appearances, referencing `{token}` so NSH's `token()` handles light/dark switching) plus sparse `finishes.light` and `finishes.dark` overrides, and `finish.<role>` merges them and resolves every `{token}` (including `oklch(from {card} l c h / 0.78)`) for the active appearance."],[667,"A fixed body"],[407,"A flat surface that groups related content: a header with its action, a body and a footer."],[2284,"A font file is the app's. Import it in TSX — the import registers it before the first frame, and its value is the family name the file declares, not the file name — and name that family in the theme:"],[414,"A footer that wraps"],[2037,"A formatting bar"],[1548,"A formatting toolbar"],[968,"A frame of your own around an input is a group. Do not wrap a `variant=\"ghost\"` `Input` in a `View` with a border: the ghost input keeps its own focus edge and ring, so the frame ends up with a second edge inside it that does not follow the frame's radius."],[180,"A frame that opens"],[365,"A ghost button's label that should line up with a card's edge is the same `View` with the look's\npadding on its leading side: the look carries `buttonSizes[size].pad` (12 at `default`, 16 at\n`lg`), so a `View` with `paddingLeft: buttonSizes.default.pad` around a ghost `Button` moves the\nlabel to the edge and keeps the hover fill the width of the button. The library ships no helper for\nit: the padding is the look's own."],[994,"A ghost input is the field itself: it keeps its own focus edge and ring, so it is not what a frame you draw around it wants. For an input in a frame of your own — a search field with a magnifier, a composer with a send button — use `InputGroup` instead: the group draws the frame and the input in it draws nothing, so the focus ring follows the group's radius."],[1673,"A group"],[2019,"A group is one toolbar for screen readers: one stop of the keyboard focus, arrow keys between its toggles. The default wears the inset track."],[879,"A group of choices"],[1047,"A group of keys"],[1414,"A grouped or stacked bar chart keeps its bands on the x scale and its baseline in the theme's\nborder: `paddingInner` and `paddingOuter` set the gap, `maxBandWidth` stops a wide window from\nstretching a bar."],[1622,"A hairline across the content, or down a row."],[1310,"A header of the app's own still answers Back, and knows where in the stack it is: `header` takes a\nfunction of the page, which is handed the slot the drawn header reads. `back()` is what that header's\nback button does — `onBackPress`, then `pop()` unless `popOnBack` is `false`; `pages()` is how many\nroutes the stack holds, so a header can show a back button only when there is one to go to."],[1312,"A header that is not this one at all is still built from `navPageLook()`'s words — `header()`,\n`title()` and `backGlyph()` — as any content of the app's is:"],[1311,"A header that is the one `NavPage` draws with something added to it composes `PageHeader` instead of\ndrawing the bar again: it keeps the back button, the title and the trailing slot in the header's\nlook, and Back still calls `onBackPress` before it pops."],[538,"A header with no picture"],[1567,"A hover scroll bar in the shared gallery theme."],[2114,"A hundred folders of a hundred files: 10 100 items. The tree is virtualized, so only the rows in view are made; opening a folder adds its hundred rows without a stall. Give it a height (the tree scrolls inside it) and keep `expanded` and `selected` as signals if you read them."],[1388,"A jump menu"],[1055,"A key cap in a button says what its shortcut is; the label is the button's own type\n(`buttonLabel`), the caps are the keys."],[1042,"A key of a shortcut, alone or in a group."],[1801,"A label above a value for metric grids."],[1072,"A label beside a control that cannot be used dims to half. Set `disabled` where the control's own\n`disabled` is set."],[1073,"A label is a row, so an icon or a switch can sit before its text; a control beside it is the\ncaller's own view, a gap of the caller's `style` apart."],[887,"A label is not associated with its control: NSH has no `<label for>`, so a screen reader hears the\ncontrol's own `accessibilityLabel`, then the `FieldLabel` as separate text. Give the control the\nsame words: `<Input accessibilityLabel=\"Email\" />` beside `<FieldLabel>Email</FieldLabel>`. A\ncontrol that carries its own label (`<Checkbox>Hard disks</Checkbox>`) needs neither."],[1404,"A label on a tile"],[1298,"A large title"],[2109,"A large tree"],[998,"A least height of your own is how the box starts taller than one line, since `style` goes over the look's — 64 points:"],[889,"A legend one step smaller over a group of checkboxes or switches: `variant=\"label\"`."],[1100,"A line in a conversation that is not a message: a status, a note, a labeled separator."],[916,"A line or two: 12 points of padding and 4 between rows."],[1437,"A line or two: 12 points of padding and 4 between rows."],[1132,"A line under the label"],[1731,"A line, an avatar, a button"],[1549,"A link bubble"],[1333,"A link in a panel"],[1571,"A list"],[778,"A list of actions"],[1595,"A list of choices from a field trigger, on the shared listbox look."],[1520,"A list that reloads when it is pulled. `refreshing` is the app's: it is set in `onRefresh` and cleared when the reload ends, and the indicator draws it."],[616,"A long list"],[1018,"A long list"],[965,"A magnifier before the control, the work after it: a spinner while the query settles, then a button\nthat clears it. Search on what the field settles on, not on every key — 300 ms after the last one\nis enough, and the spinner says the search is under way. Put the matches under the field, with the\nletters that matched in the accent, so the answer reads as the field's."],[363,"A main action and a menu half in one silhouette."],[1109,"A marker that acts"],[1115,"A marker that is still going takes a `Spinner` in `MarkerIcon`. NSH has no `status` role and no\nlive-region prop, so a marker whose change must be announced takes `role=\"alert\"`, the one it has\nfor this."],[628,"A menu at the pointer, on the shared menu look."],[1126,"A menu of items, separators and submenus on the shared menu look."],[952,"A message composer"],[394,"A month of days to pick a date or a range, on the shared calendar look."],[2201,"A native menu draws its own items, so it takes an image rather than a view: an item's `icon` is what `<Image>`'s `src` takes, fitted to the menu's icon size. `iconImage` writes a lucide node as that image, stroked in `currentColor`, so the menu draws it in its own text color:"],[1550,"A node in the text"],[129,"A note and no title"],[130,"A note of the app's own"],[121,"A notice of the whole page or form, read out when it appears."],[165,"A number (`16 / 9`) or CSS's own ratio (`\"16 / 9\"`) say the same; `\"auto\"` lets the height follow\nthe content instead."],[709,"A page of its own"],[1305,"A page's `title` is the system header's on iOS and Android, and on the desktops and the web — which\nhave no system header — it is the one `NavPage` draws: a bar's height (the theme's tallest height\nstep, and never under 48 points — shadcn's `h-12`), on the page's fill, a hairline under it, the\ntitle the theme's `title` type role semibold and the room between the back button and the trailing\nslot. It takes the roles the theme keeps for a header wherever it comes from\n(`header.headerStyle`: the page's fill, the text color, the accent for the back button's tint)."],[531,"A page's header that shrinks into a bar as the page scrolls, its picture sliding under the title."],[770,"A panel from the bottom edge, with a grabber, that a swipe closes."],[1637,"A panel that slides in from an edge of the screen over a scrim."],[1339,"A panel: `NavigationMenuContent` mounts `nsh/base` Popover on the\nshared popup surface (`popupSurface`: one edge, the menu tier's shadow, the theme's radius) 10 points\nof room inside (12 on a touch screen), placed against its trigger — `bottom` and `start` unless\n`placement`, `align` and `offset` say otherwise — so it takes that trigger's width unless `width`\nnames another. What goes in it is the app's: a grid of links, a card\nwith a description, a form."],[2200,"A part that takes an icon as a prop, such as `SelectTrigger`'s `icon` or `MarkerIcon`'s, takes the lucide node itself (`icon={Calendar}`) and draws it in its own size and color."],[999,"A password field that says what it asks for and how far the reader has got: four segments, one for\neach rule the password meets, the word for the score, and the rules under the field — a dot until\nit is met, a tick on the tone that says it is. Score the field as it is typed, and score nothing as\nnothing: an empty field asks for a password rather than calling it weak."],[990,"A password with a strength meter"],[1454,"A picture"],[221,"A picture that fails to load leaves the initials on the ring, as a gone picture should."],[158,"A picture that keeps its shape"],[1884,"A pill track or underlined row with a traveling indicator."],[2053,"A popover-coloured hint bubble on any element's `tooltip` prop."],[1424,"A popup panel that grows from its trigger, on the shared menu surface."],[1341,"A press opens and closes a panel (Return or Space with the keyboard on the trigger), and on a desktop\nthe pointer's rest opens one too: 200 ms on a trigger (`delayDuration`), and the panel closes 300 ms\nafter the pointer has left both the trigger and the panel (`closeDelay`) — time to cross from one to\nthe other, not to browse. `Infinity` turns that off: only presses and the keyboard, or only Escape and\na press outside. A touch screen has no pointer to rest, so a tap opens and closes there."],[1464,"A press sinks a pressable to 98%. `scale` is that much and nothing else: `0.8` for a dot that gives under a thumb, `1` for a row that should not move. Nothing sinks with reduced motion, or in a theme that draws no press (`press.scale` of 1)."],[1463,"A pressable is not an on / off control: `role=\"switch\"` does not make one a switch, and it has no\n`checked` — its state is `selected`. For a setting, use `Switch`, which owns the switch role and\nits value for screen readers — either the whole row (the switch's children are its label, so a tap\nanywhere toggles it) or a `Field` with the switch at the end of the row. See `Switch`."],[906,"A preview card that grows from its trigger, on the shared popover surface."],[1902,"A program's output on the theme's field surface, the keys typed back to it."],[1093,"A quiet link sits in a footer: muted, with no resting underline."],[948,"A radius of your own"],[1982,"A raised banner with a title, optional description, action and close."],[686,"A range"],[160,"A ratio written as CSS does"],[1908,"A real program"],[2087,"A reasoning line counts while the model thinks (`status=\"running\"`: a `Spinner` in the icon slot, the title pulsing) and then reads what it took. The pulse stands still when the user asks for less motion. It opens onto the text. `open` with `onOpenChange` lets the app open it when the thought is done."],[969,"A ref on the control focuses it from code — a shortcut, a request to focus a draft. The ref is the control's, not the group's."],[1462,"A region says what it is with `role` (`role=\"button\"` is the default, `role=\"link\"`, `role=\"tab\"`, …): that is NSH's own name for it, and React Native's `accessibilityRole` is not it. What a region _is_ — a link, a key in a toolbar — is the `role`; its state is `selected` and `disabled`, the base button's own props, which screen readers say. There is no `accessibilityState` prop: React Native's state names are not NSH's."],[1448,"A region that takes presses and adds only their feedback."],[1453,"A region with its own look"],[1268,"A reply arrives a few characters at a time. While it does, `TextView` takes\n`options={{ streaming: true }}` and a `streamingCaret` — a `View` with `textViewCaretClass`, the\ntheme's brand-colored block that pulses — where the next word will land. Before the first word\nthere is no text to hang the caret on, so draw the caret alone in a line as tall as the reply's\ntext and leave the view out."],[1256,"A reply that is still arriving"],[1770,"A ring with its top arc in `tint`, turning once every 800 ms; its stroke is an eighth of its side, at least 1.5 points.\nA groove behind the arc is the theme's track form to say: under the sunk form the rest of the ring follows\n`tint` at a quarter, under the tint and hairline forms there is none."],[1363,"A rolling figure"],[207,"A round picture or name-colored initials on the identity ring."],[1670,"A row"],[1809,"A row"],[2113,"A row is `treeRowHeight` tall: 28 points with a pointer, 44 on a touch screen. A well that should be\nas tall as its first rows, with the rest scrolling, sets its own height from it (`Tree`'s `style`),\nplus the list's 6 points of padding on each side."],[1693,"A row keeps its name only while the column is open: nothing else moves, so a look is written once\nwhere the row draws. A row the app draws hands its look the column's fold — `open` and `collapsible`,\nthe app's own signals — and its name goes; a look given no fold options draws the open row, and an\naction or a badge inside a folded item leaves the tree."],[1009,"A row of a list: what sits before its content, a title with a note, and controls after it."],[1162,"A row of menus whose triggers stand in one bar, as a desktop app's menu bar."],[932,"A row of photographs, each a `Pressable` that sets the image; one viewer serves them all."],[837,"A row of quick emoji beside the picker is the same pressables in a pill: six of them, a divider,\nthen a `Popover` whose trigger is a plus, with the same `EmojiPanel` inside it and `react` as its\n`onSelect`."],[2021,"A row of single-select capsules is a recipe, not a part. shadcn/ui has no capsule axis on `ToggleGroup`; where it wants one it puts `rounded-full` on the items. Here the same call-site `style` does it: `inset={false}` drops the track, `gap` spaces the chips, and `borderRadius: 999` makes the capsule. A capsule is geometry, not a theme role (`shape.control` is the corner of a badge), so the radius is the full one. The base group holds no value: the app presses the chip asked for and keeps the chosen one pressed. For a row that scrolls, put the group in a horizontal `Scroll`."],[1326,"A row of triggers, each opening a panel of anything under the bar — one at a time."],[1674,"A row that is still coming"],[1017,"A row that takes presses"],[1169,"A row with its key equivalent"],[1844,"A row with its label is the target: put the label in `children` and let `listRow` draw the row."],[1412,"A row's chart is a `Plot` with `interactive={false}`: no cross line, no hover dot, just the line. The\nwash under it is `plotWash`, the previous close a dashed `Grid` at that value with\n`plotReference`, and the newest point a second `Plot` over the first — only the last row in it — so\nits `Dot` can be a ring around a solid centre."],[1139,"A row's height in the size table is its least: give it a `View` holding its label and a note under it (`menuItemNote`) and the row grows to hold both, growing over the theme's row inset so it never touches the row after it. A neighbour with one line keeps the one-line height."],[951,"A search field"],[609,"A search palette with sectioned, virtualized rows."],[575,"A searchable field with a list of choices and chips for the values chosen, on the shared listbox look."],[677,"A selection of several is the app's too: set `selectOnModifierPress={false}`, keep your own range or\nset in `onRowPress` / `onColumnPress` (each reports Shift, Control, Option and the clicks), and paint\nit with `rowClasses`, `cellClasses` or the columns' `headerClass` / `cellClass`."],[259,"A separator is a chevron; its children replace it (a slash, a dot, a small icon)."],[1632,"A separator is usually a line of the layout: `decorative` is `true` by default, and a screen reader\nwalks from one group of content to the next without stopping on it. `decorative={false}` keeps it\nin the accessibility tree, as a `role=\"group\"` (`nsh` has no `separator` role — the nearest is a\nboundary between groups); give it an `accessibilityLabel` saying what it divides."],[253,"A separator of your own"],[872,"A set of fields under a legend: a label, its control, a description and errors that are read out."],[1837,"A settings row"],[1652,"A sheet of filters edits a draft, and the footer decides whether the page sees it. Copy the applied\nfilters in when the sheet opens, keep the draft in the content, and write it back only in the\nfooter's button; closing any other way throws the draft away."],[1203,"A short thread, and an arrival"],[1532,"A sidebar"],[2017,"A size is a step of the theme's own scales: its `side` the three heights (`dimension.size.height`),\nits `icon` the three icons (`dimension.icon`), its label the `detail`, `body` and `label` type roles.\nThe default theme's heights are 24 / 28 / 32 points with a pointer and 32 / 40 / 48 on a touch\nscreen, where a hit slop makes the target 44 points."],[354,"A size is compact with a pointer (24 / 28 / 34 points) and roomier on a touch screen (36 / 40 / 48), where a hit slop makes the target 44 points. `sm`, `default` and `lg` are the only values: a pixel size is a geometry prop (`width`, `side`), not a scale."],[1735,"A skeleton has no size of its own: the box is the caller's `style`. A line is as tall as the type\nit will hold and as wide as that text runs; an avatar or an icon is a square, round with\n`borderRadius` half its side."],[444,"A slide that holds a photograph draws its caption itself: the theme has no text role that reads\nover an arbitrary picture, so the ink is `on-media` (white in