ed`, and the root's `disabled` still keeps\nthe card closed."],[108,"A string child is a `Button`; `variant` picks its look (`secondary` by default). Anything else is\ndrawn as given."],[597,"A string child is a `Button`; anything else is drawn as given. `testID` reaches the\nbutton or the default toggle, and `accessibilityLabel` names it (the default toggle is a\nchevron with no text of its own)."],[2068,"A string child is a `Button`; anything else is the element itself, and its own press, hover\nand name stay."],[1175,"A string child is a row's label; a label and a note at the row's trailing edge (`MenubarShortcut`,\nwhich is `MenuShortcut`) are written out — the note takes the rest of the row:"],[358,"A string child is also the button's name for screen readers — they read the text it draws — so `<Button onPress={retry}>Retry</Button>` needs nothing else: a test finds it as `role:button[Retry]`. `accessibilityLabel` still wins when the name should differ from the visible text. Children drawn as given are named by the texts inside them; a button that draws no text needs the `accessibilityLabel` yourself, and an `IconButton` or `RoundButton` (an icon and no text) will not compile without one."],[1144,"A string child is drawn as the button's label in the theme's look (`variant`, `ghost` by default;\n`size` as `Button`'s). It is not a `Button` inside: `nsh/base` makes the button's own view the\npressable on the desktops, and a nested one would take the press. A single element child is the\ntrigger itself, with no wrapper: its look, `testID`, name, own press and hover styles stay, and\n`nsh/base` adds the open state to it (`expandedStyle`, `expanded:` classes)."],[1461,"A string child is plain text, and its text is the region's name for screen readers unless `accessibilityLabel` says otherwise. Children drawn as given are named by the texts inside them; one that draws no text needs an `accessibilityLabel` (the icon-only case is an `IconButton`, where it is required)."],[1096,"A string child is the link's name for screen readers too, so `<Link href=\"/docs\">Documentation</Link>` needs nothing else; `accessibilityLabel` wins when the name should differ from the text. Children drawn as given are named by the texts inside them."],[471,"A string child is the row's label and its name for screen readers, so the box and its text are one control to a reader; `accessibilityLabel` wins when it should differ. Children drawn as given are named by the texts inside them."],[1145,"A string child is the row's label and its name for screen readers; `accessibilityLabel` wins when it should differ. Children drawn as given are named by the texts inside them."],[1505,"A string child is the row's label and its name for screen readers; `accessibilityLabel` wins when it should differ. Children drawn as given are named by the texts inside them."],[1691,"A string child is the row's name. Anything else is drawn as given, which is how an icon leads a name:\nthe look has the words for it."],[2022,"A string child is the toggle's name for screen readers, and `accessibilityLabel` wins when it should differ from the visible label. Children drawn as given (an icon and a label of your own) are named by the texts inside them."],[732,"A string child of `DialogTrigger` or `DialogClose` is a `Button`; `variant` picks its look (`secondary` by default). Anything else is drawn as given."],[357,"A string is the label, and a child that changes (`{count()}` beside other children) is followed the same way. Anything else is drawn as given; `buttonLabel` and `buttonGlyph` give it the button's type and icon size."],[661,"A striped table with sortable, resizable columns."],[1672,"A sub menu"],[458,"A sunk box that fills in citrus, with a check or a dash springing in."],[1356,"A sunk number field with − / + or stacked chevron steppers."],[1742,"A sunk rail, citrus fill, and round thumb with a focus ring."],[1491,"A sunk ring that fills in citrus, with a dot growing in — alone or as a choice tile."],[1947,"A sunk well for a time of day, one segment at a time."],[633,"A surface"],[2271,"A surface inside another takes a concentric corner from its outer one: `rounded(outer, inset)` gives `outer − inset`, never below 0. `rounded()` also puts the corner on with `shape.curve` (`\"continuous\"` or `\"circular\"`):"],[775,"A swipe closes it"],[1845,"A switch is the whole row here — its children are its label, so a tap anywhere on the row toggles it — and it is read as a switch that is on or off, because `Switch` sets its own role and its own value. Nothing else has to say either."],[1856,"A switch's knob is a circle on every theme: WinUI's `ring` handle still gives its switch a round\nknob, and the family whose numbers it uses is the theme's own — the library's disc (above) or\nWinUI's stretching knob, with Material's growing knob wherever the theme tints its track (`tint`).\nThe look reads `control()` where it draws, so switching the theme redraws the track and the knob on\nthe next frame."],[768,"A tab as `renderTab` and `renderDragPreview` receive it is `DockTabValue`: `nsh/base`'s `DockTab`, named so here because `DockTab` is the component the tab is drawn with."],[286,"A tail"],[1056,"A terminal draws no cap: a key is its name in the monospaced face, one Cell row high, with no fill\nand no edge."],[861,"A terminal draws the block as its lines: no padding, one Cell row between the words and the\nactions, the title and the description as plain text."],[1906,"A terminal in the page"],[1913,"A terminal is a stream of bytes in and out; the app owns both ends."],[982,"A text field — sunk, ghost, multiline, masked, or invalid."],[1084,"A text link with underline motion, on the shared nav look."],[2388,"A theme is one `AppTheme`: `theme.json` (base tokens: colours, three `radius-*` layer lengths, and fonts),\n`semantics.json` (cross-platform semantics: style groups and `shared` + sparse `light` / `dark`\nfinishes, referencing tokens with `{token}`) and